WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.901
-Our first guest tonight,

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:03.803
is a Daytime Emmy-winning
talk show host

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:05.839
and actress who you can
see weekdays on

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.173
"Live with Kelly and Ryan."

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.441
Her book, "Live Wire:
Long-Winded Short Stories"

00:00:09.441 --> 00:00:11.044
is available now.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:13.946
our very good friend
Kelly Ripa, everybody.

00:00:13.946 --> 00:00:15.615
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:23.456 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:31.330 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:34.533
Hello, Kelly.
-Hello.

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:35.801
That was exciting, Fred.

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:37.003
Oh, my gosh.
-I know, right?

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:38.571
Having Fred here
is a big deal for us.

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:39.739
Yes, very big deal.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:41.040
-It's a big deal having you here
as well,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.843
because we hold you in very high
regard in these parts.

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:46.445
You were a week one guest with
"Late Night with Seth Meyers."

00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:47.780
And that means a lot to us

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.282
that you came here when we
were just getting started.

00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:50.383
-Guess what?

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:53.519
You have been very
instrumental in my life.

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:58.190
And I am always here for you any
time you want me, day or night,

00:00:58.190 --> 00:00:59.892
24/7.
-Thank you.

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:00.960
I can deliver your next baby.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:01.994
Whatever you want.
-Oh, that's so ---

00:01:01.994 --> 00:01:03.329
You know,
I'm glad you mentioned that.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.998
Because when our second child --

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:07.367
when our first child was born,
you sent a very conventional

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.635
and lovely, I should say, baby
gift.

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:12.571
And then our second son
was born in the lobby

00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:13.806
of our apartment building,

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:16.242
and you sent catcher's mitts.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.777
-Yes.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:20.512
-My wife misunderstood the humor
in that gift.

00:01:20.512 --> 00:01:23.750
-She did. She wrote me this
funny note saying,

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:28.154
"Oh, dear Kelly --" the most
gracious, I mean, you have --

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:30.323
-She is a very wonderful person.
-She is so wonderful.

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:31.558
And, like, you send a gift,

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:33.326
and a thank you note
arrives the next day.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:36.262
And you think, was she waiting
for this gift?

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.798
"Dear Kelly, I thought it was so
funny that you thought

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:41.801
Seth would ever take our son
to play catch."

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:42.869
And I was like --

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:47.640
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.008
No, that's to catch
the next baby...

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:50.276
-Yeah.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.145
-...when the baby -- because
they just fall out.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:53.813
-They do just fall out.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:55.481
It is funny that she loves --

00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:57.883
the thank you notes
she writes first are the ones

00:01:57.883 --> 00:01:59.685
where she can slam me
in the middle of the night.

00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:02.121
-Exactly.
-Those are the ones
she's very eager to write.

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:04.157
Those are the most important
thank you notes.

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:05.925
Thank you for the man
that did this to me.

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:10.463
-Speaking of kids, you and Mark,
your wonderful husband --

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:11.731
empty nesters.

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:12.932
I can't believe it.

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:14.701
We're kind of empty nesters.

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:16.769
And then my mom had heart
surgery,

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.438
so now we're
living with my parents.

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:20.773
-Okay. Wow.

00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:22.508
And Mark's like,
"That didn't take long."

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:23.776
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:25.244
The nest is full again,
but with older people."

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:27.613
-What happened to our nest?
-Our nest is --

00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:29.949
Well, that's very sweet
that you would

00:02:29.949 --> 00:02:32.584
create a nice home for them
at this time.

00:02:32.584 --> 00:02:34.720
Oh my gosh, we love, I mean,
we love -- we love

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.389
collectively
all of our parents.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:41.261
And we appreciate that they
fall apart in different stages.

00:02:41.261 --> 00:02:42.528
Like, yeah.
-Right.

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:44.963
So it's not like they're all
with us all at once.

00:02:44.963 --> 00:02:47.499
It's like first my Mother-in-Law
will have surgery.

00:02:47.499 --> 00:02:49.235
Then my Father-in-Law
will have surgery.

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:51.571
Then my Dad has surgery.
-It's nice when they stagger it.

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:54.040
-They stagger the surgeries.
-It's very sweet they do that.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.709
-They're thoughtful
elderly people.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.078
-So you wrote this book,

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:03.248
and this is
a collection of essays,

00:03:03.248 --> 00:03:05.618
but you know, personal
essays about your life.

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:06.986
What inspired you to do it?

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:09.221
Was it easier than you thought
it would be? Harder?

00:03:09.221 --> 00:03:12.091
-It was ten times harder than I
thought it would be.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:15.695
I thought because
I read books --

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:16.729
-Sure.

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.497
The thought process was,

00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:21.834
"I read books,
so how hard can it be?"

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:24.671
-You know right after page two
comes page three.

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:27.340
-Yeah, it's really hard
to write a book.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.408
-It's hard.

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:30.843
But as hard as it was to write
the book,

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:34.046
and it was brutal.
I'm not kidding.

00:03:34.046 --> 00:03:35.048
There were days where I'm like,

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:36.783
"God, I'm such a crap typer.

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:38.384
I'm just gonna write it
free hand."

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:41.654
Because the ideas are getting
away from me

00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:45.457
faster than I can
get them down on the page.

00:03:45.457 --> 00:03:49.828
The hardest part about all of
this, now that it's done,

00:03:49.828 --> 00:03:54.567
is, like, going out and selling
a book feels,

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:57.536
I don't want to
understate this,

00:03:57.536 --> 00:04:00.272
it feels so gross.
I mean, like --

00:04:00.272 --> 00:04:01.840
-Well, it's interesting,
right, because, yes,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.076
especially when it's
a book about yourself...

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:05.444
-Right. Exactly.
-...because you're saying

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:07.480
yeah, if you like what I'm
saying right here,

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.182
there's more in here.
-There's more in here.

00:04:10.182 --> 00:04:13.319
If you like all of this, wait
until you read this.

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:14.753
-But the interesting thing, you
know,

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:16.355
to go back to Mark,
you're obviously,

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:17.823
you write about
how you guys met.

00:04:17.823 --> 00:04:21.026
You also note that, you know,
you're still happily married,

00:04:21.026 --> 00:04:24.030
but he has different memories
about everything.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.631
He has no memories
about anything

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:29.301
because men have an ability
to turn off their memory.

00:04:29.301 --> 00:04:31.270
And I am like an elephant.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.373
I wander around
just gathering memories

00:04:34.373 --> 00:04:36.609
that I wish I could forget.
-Weighing you down.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:39.278
-It's weighing me down.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:41.513
No, it's really --
it's like a very --

00:04:41.513 --> 00:04:43.348
like I told you back stage

00:04:43.348 --> 00:04:45.785
I said, "Can't we just come out
here and talk about

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:47.720
'I'm not scared, you're scared."
-My children's book. Thank you.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:48.854
Because I'd rather
talk about your book.

00:04:48.854 --> 00:04:50.522
-I think everybody
can go out and buy it.

00:04:50.522 --> 00:04:52.058
We don't have to talk about it.

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:53.425
-Guys --
-We don't have to talk about it.

00:04:53.425 --> 00:04:55.294
-Guys, we don't want to brag,
there's a bundle deal.

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:59.131
If you buy both books,
you get a discount.

00:04:59.131 --> 00:05:02.568
-You do talk about --
so you met Mark on

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:05.104
"All My Children."
-I met Mark on "All My Children"

00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:09.808
in such a way that today,
in this atmosphere,

00:05:09.808 --> 00:05:14.480
this climate,
that we all live in,

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:19.452
I basically,
accidentally, innocently,

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:21.553
sexually harassed my husband.
-Okay.

00:05:21.553 --> 00:05:23.155
Very big of you to admit.

00:05:23.155 --> 00:05:26.225
-Yes, well, I admit it right
there on the page.

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:30.129
I walked up to him at a screen
test and I said,

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:33.432
"I had a dream about you
that we --"

00:05:33.432 --> 00:05:36.235
I know. At that point,
he should have hit the button

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:38.170
and they would just take me out.
-Right.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:42.074
-But I said, "I had a dream
about you.

00:05:42.074 --> 00:05:46.712
We were on a plane together
to Rome and we had a baby.

00:05:46.712 --> 00:05:49.248
The baby was wearing
red feeted pajamas."

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:51.250
And he's like, "Oh, yeah?"

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.051
[ Laughter ]

00:05:53.051 --> 00:05:54.820
Like, he's looking around at
the other guys that are

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.823
screen-testing like, "Has she
said anything bat[bleep]

00:05:57.823 --> 00:06:00.393
like this to any of you?"
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.393 --> 00:06:02.194
And the other guys
are all listening.

00:06:02.194 --> 00:06:04.630
And he goes, "Oh, have you ever
been to Rome?"

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.265
And I said, "No, never."

00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:09.902
And he goes, "Oh, we used to
live in Italy.

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:13.172
You would like it." Not knowing
me at all. And he walks away.

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:15.341
He walks away probably
to file a restraining order.

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:16.976
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:21.547
And then I was like,
"who is we? Who is we?"

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:22.982
-Right, when "we" used to
live there.

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:26.318
-Yes, so I made it my mission
to figure out who "we" was.

00:06:26.318 --> 00:06:28.387
-Uh-huh.
-Meaning his parents

00:06:28.387 --> 00:06:29.921
and his siblings.
-Yeah.

00:06:29.921 --> 00:06:31.157
-Because he was seven.

00:06:31.157 --> 00:06:33.259
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:35.160
But I thought he had a wife.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.129
-He goes, "I have a dream that
one day,

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:38.430
they're going to be
living with us."

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.299
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly.

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:43.135
-"You'll love them."
-"And I think you're the girl

00:06:43.135 --> 00:06:45.671
to make that dream come true."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:49.708
-You also talk about how
this business, that I'm sure is

00:06:49.708 --> 00:06:51.978
true of all businesses,
but how much this business

00:06:51.978 --> 00:06:54.446
has changed for women...
-Yes.

00:06:54.446 --> 00:06:58.084
-...over the course of your --
at this point, very, you know,

00:06:58.084 --> 00:07:00.686
you've had a long career. You've
been in this for a long time.

00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:03.956
How do you feel --
What is your main takeaway

00:07:03.956 --> 00:07:07.960
for how much it's changed?
-I think it's -- any change

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.996
incrementally -- it always seems
so slow, right?

00:07:10.996 --> 00:07:13.532
Change seems glacial.
But when I think back to when

00:07:13.532 --> 00:07:19.138
I was starting out, you know,
lucky for me I wasn't fired for

00:07:19.138 --> 00:07:21.140
sexually harassing my husband.

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:27.346
But seriously, it was very hard
for women to advocate

00:07:27.346 --> 00:07:29.882
for themselves in any way
in terms of pay equity

00:07:29.882 --> 00:07:34.086
or having appropriate office
space or being given appropriate

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:38.057
breaks that were so readily
available to the men.

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:42.662
So I think those changes that
sort of just happen now,

00:07:42.662 --> 00:07:44.797
people forget there was a time
and a place,

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:47.700
in the not too distant past,
where that was considered

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:49.769
like some form of
insubordination.

00:07:49.769 --> 00:07:53.539
-Yeah, to ask for those things.
-"I have to nurse my baby.

00:07:53.539 --> 00:07:56.742
Can I take a break?"
Like, "Again?!

00:07:56.742 --> 00:08:00.846
How many times a day
does this kid eat?"

00:08:00.846 --> 00:08:03.716
-And then you have -- I will say
as someone who has kids,

00:08:03.716 --> 00:08:05.284
they eat all the time.
-All the time.

00:08:05.284 --> 00:08:08.888
They're like energy vampires.
-They are energy vampires.

00:08:08.888 --> 00:08:13.192
Speaking of, I, to this day,
remain amazed by your energy.

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:15.494
It is always so great to see you
on television.

00:08:15.494 --> 00:08:16.762
Even nicer to see you in person.

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:17.763
Thanks so much for being here,
Kelly.

00:08:17.763 --> 00:08:19.398
-Thank you.
I'm so happy to see you.

00:08:19.398 --> 00:08:20.900
-Kelly Ripa.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:25.504
"Live Wire: Long-Winded
Short Stories" is on sale now.

00:08:25.504 --> 00:08:26.905
We'll be right back with
Fred Armisen,

00:08:26.905 --> 00:08:28.875
Ana Fabrega and Julio Torres.

